        <p>Two things stand out. First, WebP was smaller than JPEG on both files, by roughly 60% on the illustration and 55% on the screenshot, which tracks with what Google's own WebP documentation claims. Second, and less expected: choosing PNG as the output format did not preserve the original file size, it made both files larger. That is worth its own explanation, which I cover in <a href="/blog/why-png-reencode-bigger/">why re-saving a PNG can make the file bigger</a>.</p>

